  "query_preview": "/Monday through Friday/...\nIssued 251 PM MST FRI NOV 27 2026\nA shortwave trough passing across the area on Monday will bring\nthe next shot of widespread precipitation across the area. The \nsoutherly flow precursing this trough will result in initial \nsnow levels of 6.5-8.5kft MSL, dropping down to 5.5-7kft MSL \nMonday afternoon. These snow levels will limit any significant \nsnow accumulations to higher terrain in the West Central Idaho \nMountains. Some high elevation passes will likely experience \nwinter driving conditions Monday morning into Monday afternoon, \nwith 6 to 8 inches of snow forecast for Highway 21 near and on \nBanner Summit. Monday will also feature gusty conditions, with \ngusts to 25-35 kt south and west of the Snake River Plain and \nalong SW-Idaho ridge tops. \n\nCome Tuesday, the low that sent the previous shortwave through\nopen and phase with a deeper low approaching the SoCal coast. \nFlow becoming increasingly southwesterly will steer a plume of \nmoisture northward. As of now it looks like the bulk of this \nmoisture will remain south and east of our CWA, although it will\nallow for a 30- 40% chance of precipitation for the western \nMagic Valley and Owyhee/Camas Counties. While some uncertainty \nremains in the exact track of the SoCal low, ensembles favor it \nprogressing through the Desert Southwest. This track will keep \nour area out of the deformation zone and dry mid-week as a \npositively tilted trough builds over our area. Uncertainty \nincreases late week-next week, although most guidance resolves a\nweak shortwave crossing over Thursday into Friday. Moisture \navailability is in question with this shortwave, with low \nprecipitation chances, although at the very least it will bring \nincreased cloud cover. Temperatures through the long-term period\nwill remain near or slightly above normal.",
